{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Debug-orchestrator follow-up: Defect 2's fix is CORRECT but NOT COMPLETE IN SCOPE. Filed `bff5-9163-cddd-4158` (herbal-garish-monkey), linked discovered_from this ticket.\n\n## What I verified\n\nBoth disk alarms now read `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"` on main (monitoring_autodeploy.tf root_disk_pressure, monitoring.tf gerrit_data_disk_high). Defect 2's landed fix is confirmed present in the tree.\n\n## What the fix left behind\n\nThose were TWO alarms. Eleven others in the SAME `rebar/host` namespace are still `notBreaching`, and I checked whether that is actually correct rather than assuming from the metric names. It is not.\n\nThe inline Terraform comments justify notBreaching with a claim about the publisher — 'No X errors published in a period is the healthy steady state'. The publisher contradicts it. `infra/scripts/observability.sh` implements every host counter as an OFFSET-DELTA: it greps a cumulative journal, subtracts a persisted offset, and unconditionally publishes the delta (`--value \"$new\"`), which is `0` when nothing happened. `publish_autodeploy_marker_delta` (~line 296) is the shared helper for six of them; the probe is a 5-minute systemd timer.\n\nSo a healthy period publishes `0`, not nothing. Missing data means the publisher is dead — the exact inversion this ticket documented. Live CloudWatch confirms: sampling 2026-09-01 12:00-14:00Z at 5-min periods returned dense datapoints, all 0.0, for all eleven.\n\nWorst case is `mirror_out_of_sync`, which is broken beyond host death: its publish sits inside `if [ -n \"$gerrit_sha\" ] && [ -n \"$github_sha\" ]` and the else branch publishes NOTHING, so a LIVE healthy host whose Gerrit REST call or git ls-remote breaks mutes the alarm forever while GitHub main drifts. monitoring_ws7.tf states no other alarm covers mirror drift.\n\nThe residual uncovered case for all of them is the probe dying while the host lives — an observability.timer failure, IAM revocation, or `aws` erroring — which mutes ten alarms at once with nothing else firing.\n\nOne genuine exception: `bridge_run_failures` is also a heartbeat but is the only one whose tradeoff is explicitly named and compensated (monitoring_bridge.tf:40-45 assigns the stop-entirely case to the Reconciler Heartbeat Canary). And `monitoring_eb6e.tf:109` (Bedrock) is correctly notBreaching and out of scope — an AWS-published metric genuinely absent when idle.\n\nTuning constraint recorded on bff5: five of the eleven are evaluation_periods=1/datapoints_to_alarm=1 and would FLAP on normal timer jitter (~22 of 24 periods present in a 2h sample), so any flip must be paired with evaluation_periods >= 3, matching how root_disk_pressure is configured.\n\n## Remaining on THIS ticket\n\nAC3 is measured. AC4 (bounded ceiling) and AC5 (/var/gerrit) are now owned by epic `6202-e1c7-c57f-4897` (endowed-upset-scaup), per the operator-approved split recorded above — that epic exists, is linked discovered_from here, and currently has NO CHILDREN, so it still needs decomposition. Genuinely outstanding on this bug: AC1's induced-publish-stop verification, AC2's replay of the recorded series against the new config, and AC6's runbook (no wedged-host/SSM-ConnectionLost entry exists in infra/runbooks/ today; I have one being drafted)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788400548029770001,"uuid":"b84da71e-8141-46a1-99c5-faacfa2562e1"}